## Scope

<One paragraph: what's INSIDE this view's boundary, what's OUTSIDE. The most important section in this file — if scope is fuzzy, the view will accumulate cross-cutting content and stop being progressively-loadable. Be ruthless: if a topic touches multiple contexts equally, it belongs in `system.md` cross-cutting glossary, not here.>

## Glossary (terms owned by this view)

> **Ownership rule** (the discipline that makes views work): every term has exactly *one* owning view (or `system.md` if genuinely cross-cutting). Other views *use* this term but never *redefine* it. `lex-audit` flags any term defined in >1 view.
>
> If a term you'd put here is also load-bearing in another view, it probably belongs in `system.md` cross-cutting glossary instead. When in doubt, ask "does this term make sense without the rest of this view's context?" — if yes, it's cross-cutting.

- **<Term>**: <definition>. NOT to be confused with <similar term in another view>.
- **<Term>**: <definition>.

## References (terms used here but owned elsewhere)

> *Optional.* Use only when load-order matters — a reader skimming this view alone might hit an undefined term. Pointer-only; never redefine.

- **<Term>** — owned by `lexicon/system.md` Glossary (or `lexicon/views/<other-view>.md`).
- **<Term>** — owned by `lexicon/views/<other-view>.md`.

## Invariants

> Properties that must hold *within this context*. Cross-context invariants live in `system.md`. If you find an invariant being asserted here that other contexts also depend on, promote it to `system.md`.

- **<Invariant name>**: <plain-language statement>. <Why it matters; what breaks if violated.>
- **<Invariant name>**: <...>

